WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:05.176 --> 00:05.776 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

00:06.596 --> 00:08.926 align:middle
Good afternoon, and welcome.

00:12.286 --> 00:14.586 align:middle
My colleagues at the Federal
Reserve and I are dedicated

00:14.586 --> 00:15.866 align:middle
to serving the American people.

00:17.056 --> 00:19.736 align:middle
We do this by steadfastly
pursuing the goals

00:19.736 --> 00:20.956 align:middle
that Congress has given us:

00:21.526 --> 00:23.536 align:middle
maximum employment
and stable prices.

00:24.536 --> 00:27.146 align:middle
We're committed to making the
best decisions we can based

00:27.146 --> 00:29.026 align:middle
on facts and objective analysis.

00:30.456 --> 00:32.716 align:middle
Today we decided to
lower the interest rates

00:32.716 --> 00:33.956 align:middle
for the third time this year.

00:34.446 --> 00:37.956 align:middle
We took this step to help
keep the U.S. economy strong

00:37.956 --> 00:39.956 align:middle
in the face of global
developments

00:39.956 --> 00:42.796 align:middle
and to provide some insurance
against ongoing risks.

00:43.996 --> 00:46.626 align:middle
As I will explain shortly, the
policy adjustments we have made

00:46.626 --> 00:49.446 align:middle
since last year are
providing-and will continue

00:49.446 --> 00:52.106 align:middle
to provide-meaningful
support to the economy.

00:53.196 --> 00:55.636 align:middle
We believe that monetary
policy is in a good place.

00:56.156 --> 01:00.536 align:middle
The U.S. economy is in its
11th year of expansion,

01:01.126 --> 01:03.326 align:middle
and the baseline outlook
remains favorable.

01:03.806 --> 01:08.116 align:middle
The overall economy is
growing at a moderate rate.

01:08.896 --> 01:11.076 align:middle
Household spending
continues to be strong,

01:11.076 --> 01:14.376 align:middle
supported by a healthy job
market, rising incomes,

01:14.376 --> 01:16.086 align:middle
and solid consumer confidence.

01:16.986 --> 01:19.856 align:middle
In contrast, business investment
and exports remain weak,

01:20.696 --> 01:23.356 align:middle
and manufacturing output has
declined over the past year.

01:24.646 --> 01:27.586 align:middle
Sluggish growth abroad and trade
developments have been weighing

01:27.586 --> 01:28.466 align:middle
on those sectors.

01:29.656 --> 01:32.506 align:middle
Looking ahead, we continue to
expect the economy to expand

01:32.506 --> 01:35.296 align:middle
at a moderate rate, reflecting
solid household spending

01:35.296 --> 01:36.856 align:middle
and supportive financial
conditions.

01:38.796 --> 01:40.156 align:middle
The job market remains strong.

01:40.466 --> 01:43.636 align:middle
The unemployment rate has
been near half-century lows

01:43.636 --> 01:45.036 align:middle
for a year and a half.

01:45.336 --> 01:47.426 align:middle
The pace of job gains
has eased this year

01:47.426 --> 01:50.166 align:middle
but has remained solid; we
had expected some slowing

01:50.166 --> 01:51.986 align:middle
after last year's strong pace.

01:53.176 --> 01:55.506 align:middle
Participation in the
labor force by people

01:55.506 --> 01:57.856 align:middle
in their prime working
years has been increasing,

01:58.676 --> 02:00.726 align:middle
and wages have been
rising, particularly

02:00.726 --> 02:02.196 align:middle
for lower-paying jobs.

02:03.426 --> 02:04.856 align:middle
People who live and work in low-

02:04.856 --> 02:07.616 align:middle
and middle-income
communities tell us that many

02:07.616 --> 02:10.186 align:middle
who have struggled to find work
are now getting opportunities

02:10.186 --> 02:12.506 align:middle
to add new and better
chapters to their lives.

02:13.256 --> 02:15.866 align:middle
This underscores for
us the importance

02:15.936 --> 02:17.246 align:middle
of sustaining the expansion

02:18.046 --> 02:20.006 align:middle
so that the strong job
market reaches more

02:20.006 --> 02:21.016 align:middle
of those left behind.

02:23.416 --> 02:26.246 align:middle
Inflation continues to
run below our symmetric 2

02:26.246 --> 02:27.086 align:middle
percent objective.

02:28.286 --> 02:29.966 align:middle
Over the 12 months
through August,

02:30.416 --> 02:33.366 align:middle
total PCE inflation
was 1.4 percent

02:34.156 --> 02:36.236 align:middle
and core inflation
was 1.8 percent.

02:37.526 --> 02:40.396 align:middle
Inflation pressures remain
muted, and indicators

02:40.396 --> 02:43.196 align:middle
of longer-term inflation
expectations are

02:43.196 --> 02:45.176 align:middle
at the lower end of
their historic ranges.

02:45.686 --> 02:49.546 align:middle
We're mindful that continued
below-target inflation could

02:49.546 --> 02:51.996 align:middle
lead to an unwelcome
downward slide

02:52.216 --> 02:54.096 align:middle
in long-term inflation
expectations.

02:54.676 --> 02:57.496 align:middle
However, against the
backdrop of a strong economy

02:57.496 --> 02:59.026 align:middle
and supportive monetary policy,

02:59.786 --> 03:02.726 align:middle
we expect inflation
will rise to 2 percent.

03:04.376 --> 03:07.046 align:middle
Overall, we continue to
see sustained expansion

03:07.046 --> 03:09.496 align:middle
of economic activity,
a strong labor market,

03:09.986 --> 03:12.546 align:middle
and inflation near our
symmetric 2 percent objective

03:12.546 --> 03:13.366 align:middle
as most likely.

03:15.136 --> 03:17.466 align:middle
While this has been our
outlook for quite some time,

03:17.466 --> 03:19.356 align:middle
our views about the
path of interest rates

03:19.746 --> 03:21.906 align:middle
that will best achieve
these outcomes have changed

03:21.976 --> 03:23.606 align:middle
significantly over
the past year.

03:24.976 --> 03:26.766 align:middle
As I mentioned, weakness
in global growth

03:26.876 --> 03:29.116 align:middle
and trade developments
have weighed on the economy

03:29.116 --> 03:30.756 align:middle
and pose ongoing risks.

03:32.066 --> 03:34.836 align:middle
These factors, in conjunction
with muted inflation pressures,

03:34.886 --> 03:36.596 align:middle
have led us to lower
our assessment

03:36.596 --> 03:37.646 align:middle
of the appropriate level

03:37.976 --> 03:40.166 align:middle
of the federal funds
rate over the past year.

03:41.186 --> 03:43.986 align:middle
In both July and September, we
reduced the target rate [range]

03:43.986 --> 03:46.356 align:middle
for the federal funds rate
by ¼ percentage point,

03:46.756 --> 03:48.406 align:middle
and we did so again today,

03:48.826 --> 03:51.496 align:middle
bringing the range
to 1½ to 1 percent.

03:52.296 --> 03:56.046 align:middle
The policy adjustments we've
made to date will continue

03:56.046 --> 03:58.576 align:middle
to provide significant
support for the economy.

03:59.976 --> 04:03.626 align:middle
Since monetary policy operates
with a lag, the full effects

04:03.626 --> 04:06.616 align:middle
of these adjustments on
economic growth, the job market,

04:06.746 --> 04:09.586 align:middle
and inflation will be
realized over time.

04:10.076 --> 04:14.036 align:middle
We see the current stance
of monetary policy as likely

04:14.036 --> 04:17.586 align:middle
to remain appropriate as
long as incoming information

04:17.586 --> 04:19.786 align:middle
about the economy
remains broadly consistent

04:19.786 --> 04:22.536 align:middle
with our outlook of
moderate economic growth,

04:23.246 --> 04:24.256 align:middle
a strong labor market,

04:24.746 --> 04:27.526 align:middle
and inflation near our
symmetric 2 percent objective.

04:27.886 --> 04:32.046 align:middle
We believe monetary
policy is in a good place

04:32.046 --> 04:33.206 align:middle
to achieve these outcomes.

04:34.296 --> 04:36.366 align:middle
Looking ahead, we'll be
monitoring the effects

04:36.366 --> 04:39.316 align:middle
of our policy actions, along
with other information bearing

04:39.316 --> 04:42.286 align:middle
on the outlook, as we
assess the appropriate path

04:42.286 --> 04:43.956 align:middle
of the target range
for the fed funds rate.

04:44.726 --> 04:46.896 align:middle
Of course, if developments
emerge

04:46.896 --> 04:49.446 align:middle
that cause a material
reassessment of our outlook,

04:49.776 --> 04:51.076 align:middle
we would respond accordingly.

04:51.846 --> 04:53.756 align:middle
Policy is not on
a preset course.

04:55.786 --> 04:57.076 align:middle
Let me end with a few words

04:57.076 --> 05:00.116 align:middle
about our technical
monetary policy operations.

05:01.966 --> 05:04.526 align:middle
In January, we made the
key decision to continue

05:04.526 --> 05:07.646 align:middle
to implement monetary policy
in an ample-reserves regime.

05:08.486 --> 05:09.776 align:middle
In that operating framework,

05:09.816 --> 05:12.196 align:middle
we control the federal
funds rate primarily

05:12.196 --> 05:13.726 align:middle
by setting our administered
rates,

05:14.006 --> 05:15.546 align:middle
not through frequent
interventions

05:15.546 --> 05:17.756 align:middle
to actively manage the
supply of reserves.

05:19.126 --> 05:21.486 align:middle
In the transition to the
efficient and effective level

05:21.486 --> 05:25.036 align:middle
of reserves in this regime,
we slowed the gradual decline

05:25.036 --> 05:27.706 align:middle
in our balance sheet in March
and we stopped it in July.

05:28.316 --> 05:31.616 align:middle
In response to the funding
pressures in money markets

05:31.656 --> 05:34.166 align:middle
that emerged in mid-September,
we concluded

05:34.166 --> 05:37.536 align:middle
that it would be appropriate to
maintain, over a time-over time,

05:37.536 --> 05:40.226 align:middle
a level of reserve balances
at or above the level

05:40.226 --> 05:42.946 align:middle
that prevailed in early
September of this year.

05:43.976 --> 05:46.976 align:middle
To achieve this ample level,
we announced on October 11

05:47.046 --> 05:49.216 align:middle
that we would purchase
Treasury bills at least

05:49.216 --> 05:51.866 align:middle
into the second quarter
of next year as well

05:51.866 --> 05:54.456 align:middle
as continue temporary
open market operations

05:54.456 --> 05:55.936 align:middle
at least through January.

05:56.996 --> 06:00.786 align:middle
These actions are
purely technical measures

06:00.826 --> 06:02.546 align:middle
to support the effective
implementation

06:02.546 --> 06:05.296 align:middle
of monetary policy as
we continue to learn

06:05.296 --> 06:07.036 align:middle
about the appropriate
level of reserves.

06:07.946 --> 06:09.226 align:middle
They do not represent a change

06:09.226 --> 06:10.786 align:middle
in the stance of
monetary policy.

06:11.556 --> 06:15.546 align:middle
In particular, our Treasury bill
purchases should not be confused

06:15.546 --> 06:17.606 align:middle
with the large-scale
asset purchase programs

06:17.606 --> 06:19.786 align:middle
that we deployed after
the financial crisis.

06:20.686 --> 06:23.436 align:middle
In those programs, we
purchased longer-term securities

06:23.436 --> 06:26.426 align:middle
to put downward pressure on
longer-term interest rates

06:26.486 --> 06:28.306 align:middle
and ease broader
financial conditions.

06:29.496 --> 06:32.016 align:middle
In contrast, increasing
the supply of reserves

06:32.016 --> 06:34.436 align:middle
by purchasing Treasury
bills only alters the mix

06:34.436 --> 06:36.266 align:middle
of short-term assets
held by the public

06:36.686 --> 06:39.266 align:middle
and should not materially
affect demand and supply

06:39.266 --> 06:40.646 align:middle
for longer-term securities

06:41.376 --> 06:42.946 align:middle
or financial conditions
more broadly.

06:43.666 --> 06:44.296 align:middle
Thank you.

06:44.466 --> 06:46.306 align:middle
I will be happy to
take your questions.

06:46.686 --> 06:48.126 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

06:48.856 --> 06:51.666 align:middle
Michael McKee with Bloomberg
Radio and Television.

06:52.096 --> 06:54.296 align:middle
So I guess the question
is, is this it?

06:54.296 --> 06:57.516 align:middle
Do you consider rates to
be accommodative enough now

06:57.516 --> 07:00.986 align:middle
to achieve your goal of
sustaining the expansion?

07:00.986 --> 07:05.046 align:middle
And as far as global Wall
Street's primary question,

07:05.046 --> 07:08.546 align:middle
what kind of change in the
economy would cause you

07:08.546 --> 07:10.056 align:middle
to reassess-some sort

07:10.056 --> 07:12.506 align:middle
of significant deterioration
in what?

07:12.906 --> 07:13.696 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

07:13.866 --> 07:16.026 align:middle
So what we've said, to repeat,

07:16.026 --> 07:18.806 align:middle
is that we see the current
stance of policy as "likely

07:18.806 --> 07:21.616 align:middle
to remain appropriate as
long as incoming information

07:21.616 --> 07:23.806 align:middle
about the economy
remains broadly consistent

07:23.946 --> 07:24.746 align:middle
with our outlook."

07:25.776 --> 07:29.306 align:middle
And we say that, really,
because both of the performance

07:29.306 --> 07:31.126 align:middle
of the economy and
the stance of policy.

07:32.036 --> 07:35.656 align:middle
The performance of the economy
has been-particularly the

07:35.656 --> 07:38.126 align:middle
household sector-has been
strong, has been resilient,

07:38.656 --> 07:41.966 align:middle
with low unemployment,
attractive levels

07:41.966 --> 07:43.816 align:middle
of job creation,
wages moving up,

07:44.316 --> 07:47.366 align:middle
labor force participation
moving up, household confidence,

07:47.476 --> 07:51.446 align:middle
and solid gains in many
measures of consumer spending.

07:51.846 --> 07:56.416 align:middle
The manufacturing sector,
particularly manufacturing,

07:56.416 --> 07:58.666 align:middle
and also investment and
exports have been weaker.

07:58.666 --> 08:00.256 align:middle
But, overall, we've
seen moderate growth,

08:00.256 --> 08:02.716 align:middle
a strong labor market,
inflation moving up.

08:02.716 --> 08:04.536 align:middle
We see the outlook as
for more of the same.

08:04.586 --> 08:08.456 align:middle
We also see the risks to the
outlook as perhaps having moved

08:08.456 --> 08:10.156 align:middle
in a positive direction
over the course

08:10.156 --> 08:11.986 align:middle
of this intermeeting
period, although,

08:11.986 --> 08:13.616 align:middle
some remain-some things
remain to be seen.

08:13.996 --> 08:14.836 align:middle
So that's the economy.

08:14.876 --> 08:17.666 align:middle
Turning to policy, we've
moved the stance of policy

08:17.666 --> 08:21.236 align:middle
over the course of the year to a
more accommodative stance, and,

08:21.236 --> 08:23.476 align:middle
after the cuts at the
last three meetings,

08:23.476 --> 08:24.866 align:middle
the federal funds
rate is now in a range

08:24.866 --> 08:27.146 align:middle
between 1½ and 1 percent.

08:27.836 --> 08:29.886 align:middle
We feel that policy
is well positioned

08:29.886 --> 08:31.986 align:middle
to support the outlook
that I described.

08:32.666 --> 08:36.136 align:middle
So you asked what it would
take, you know, to move, and,

08:36.136 --> 08:42.486 align:middle
as I mentioned, we're going
to be watching all factors,

08:42.686 --> 08:46.126 align:middle
And if developments emerge that
cause a material reassessment

08:46.126 --> 08:48.146 align:middle
of our outlook, we would
respond accordingly.

08:48.226 --> 08:49.046 align:middle
But that's what it would take:

08:49.046 --> 08:50.626 align:middle
a material reassessment
of our outlook.

08:50.656 --> 08:51.016 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

08:51.056 --> 08:53.776 align:middle
If I could follow up-if the
markets decide that you need

08:53.916 --> 08:56.306 align:middle
to cut rates again, will
you be forced to follow?

08:57.426 --> 08:57.666 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:57.726 --> 09:00.676 align:middle
We'll be looking at a full
range of data about the economy

09:00.676 --> 09:03.826 align:middle
and about the risks
to the outlook,

09:04.036 --> 09:06.736 align:middle
and I've given you a sense
of what our outlook is.

09:06.736 --> 09:09.186 align:middle
It's for moderate growth,
a strong labor market,

09:09.586 --> 09:13.076 align:middle
and inflation near our
2 percent objective.

09:13.246 --> 09:16.536 align:middle
If something happens to cause
us to materially reassess

09:16.586 --> 09:19.656 align:middle
that outlook, that's what would
cause us to change our views

09:19.816 --> 09:21.076 align:middle
on the appropriate
stance of policy.

09:21.716 --> 09:23.596 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

09:24.636 --> 09:30.516 align:middle
Hi, Heather Long from
the Washington Post.

09:30.516 --> 09:33.526 align:middle
You just said that the risks
to the outlook are moving

09:33.526 --> 09:35.346 align:middle
in a positive direction.

09:35.346 --> 09:38.546 align:middle
I'm wondering if you could
specify-is that on trade,

09:38.546 --> 09:39.756 align:middle
or are there other matters?

09:40.026 --> 09:41.846 align:middle
This morning we obviously
learned

09:41.846 --> 09:43.646 align:middle
that we've now had two quarters

09:43.646 --> 09:47.206 align:middle
of contracting business
investment, which would seem

09:47.206 --> 09:49.776 align:middle
to be moving the outlook
in the other direction.

09:50.016 --> 09:50.466 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:50.596 --> 09:52.326 align:middle
So, in terms of risks,
what I was referring

09:52.326 --> 09:55.186 align:middle
to there-the principal risks

09:55.186 --> 09:58.346 align:middle
that we've been monitoring
have been, really,

09:58.346 --> 10:02.416 align:middle
slowing global growth and trade
policy developments as well

10:02.416 --> 10:03.606 align:middle
as muted inflation pressure.

10:03.606 --> 10:07.706 align:middle
So I was really referring
there to trade developments.

10:07.706 --> 10:13.746 align:middle
We have that phase-one potential
agreement with China, which,

10:14.196 --> 10:17.486 align:middle
if signed and put into
effect, could have the effect

10:17.486 --> 10:20.786 align:middle
of reducing trade tensions
and reducing uncertainty,

10:20.786 --> 10:24.506 align:middle
and that would bode well, we
think, for business confidence

10:24.506 --> 10:26.626 align:middle
and perhaps activity over time.

10:26.716 --> 10:29.536 align:middle
So that has the potential

10:29.536 --> 10:32.476 align:middle
for being an improvement
in the risk picture.

10:32.786 --> 10:37.876 align:middle
Brexit, I would say, as
well-it appears that the risk

10:38.526 --> 10:43.606 align:middle
of a no-deal Brexit seems
to have materially declined.

10:43.606 --> 10:47.556 align:middle
I think on both situations
there's plenty of risk left,

10:47.626 --> 10:50.306 align:middle
but I'd have to say that the
risks seem to have subsided.

10:50.896 --> 10:54.046 align:middle
You asked about business
investment, and that's right.

10:54.086 --> 10:55.516 align:middle
Business investment
has been weak,

10:55.516 --> 10:57.746 align:middle
and today's reading
was weak as well.

10:58.486 --> 11:02.306 align:middle
And it was broad
across equipment

11:02.306 --> 11:06.226 align:middle
and other parts of-all parts

11:06.226 --> 11:07.826 align:middle
of business fixed
investment were weak.

11:08.286 --> 11:09.986 align:middle
That's consistent
with what we've seen,

11:10.366 --> 11:12.116 align:middle
but that's the economy
we've had this year.

11:12.116 --> 11:13.746 align:middle
What we've had is an economy

11:13.746 --> 11:18.876 align:middle
where the consumer is really
driving growth, and, you know,

11:18.996 --> 11:21.466 align:middle
personal consumption
expenditures were almost 3

11:21.466 --> 11:24.616 align:middle
percent in this quarter-in this
first reading for the quarter.

11:24.616 --> 11:28.856 align:middle
So, overall, we see the
economy as having been resilient

11:28.916 --> 11:32.086 align:middle
to the-you know, the winds that
have been blowing this year.

11:32.086 --> 11:32.376 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

11:32.666 --> 11:33.906 align:middle
Can I just follow up quickly?

11:33.906 --> 11:37.076 align:middle
I mean, there's this concern
that I sometimes hear that,

11:37.076 --> 11:40.336 align:middle
with business, businesses
have cut investment,

11:40.336 --> 11:42.586 align:middle
and are they going to turn
around and cut employment?

11:42.586 --> 11:46.036 align:middle
You know, sort of, if they
continue to be concerned.

11:46.036 --> 11:48.686 align:middle
Can you give a sense of how the
Committee is thinking-thinking

11:48.686 --> 11:49.596 align:middle
through that possibility?

11:49.596 --> 11:49.936 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:49.936 --> 11:50.996 align:middle
Yes, so that is a risk.

11:50.996 --> 11:52.356 align:middle
That's a risk that
we've been monitoring,

11:52.356 --> 11:53.546 align:middle
but we don't see it yet.

11:53.546 --> 11:56.596 align:middle
We don't see rising
initial claims or layoffs

11:56.596 --> 11:57.326 align:middle
or anything like that.

11:57.326 --> 11:59.646 align:middle
But the risk you
mention of the weakness

11:59.646 --> 12:03.566 align:middle
in the manufacturing-in export
investment, business investment,

12:03.666 --> 12:06.816 align:middle
parts of the economy getting
into the consumer side

12:06.816 --> 12:08.106 align:middle
of the economy, we
really don't see that.

12:08.106 --> 12:10.326 align:middle
What we continue to see
is good job creation.

12:10.556 --> 12:15.056 align:middle
Unemployment has declined again,
and the household survey is now

12:15.056 --> 12:17.016 align:middle
at a 50-year low-has
been, you know,

12:17.016 --> 12:19.666 align:middle
very close to 50-year
lows for 18 months now.

12:20.456 --> 12:22.536 align:middle
So it's very positive.

12:22.536 --> 12:24.556 align:middle
The consumer-facing
companies that we talk

12:24.556 --> 12:28.116 align:middle
to in our vast network of
contacts report, you know,

12:28.116 --> 12:31.446 align:middle
that consumers are doing well
and are focused on, you know,

12:31.446 --> 12:33.966 align:middle
the good job market
and rising incomes,

12:33.966 --> 12:35.206 align:middle
and that's their
principle focus.

12:35.206 --> 12:37.866 align:middle
So that is the thing that's
pushing the economy forward,

12:37.866 --> 12:40.876 align:middle
and it doesn't seem to
have been affected so far

12:40.876 --> 12:42.546 align:middle
by weakness in the other areas.

12:43.626 --> 12:44.086 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

12:44.286 --> 12:49.086 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek from
the New York Times.

12:49.086 --> 12:51.736 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

12:51.906 --> 12:56.166 align:middle
So you've previously sort of
compared this rate-cutting cycle

12:56.166 --> 12:58.276 align:middle
to the insurance
cuts in the '90s,

12:58.496 --> 13:00.156 align:middle
and in both of those instances,

13:00.716 --> 13:04.216 align:middle
the Greenspan Fed took those
cuts back after a while.

13:04.216 --> 13:06.326 align:middle
They raised rates
again fairly quickly.

13:06.326 --> 13:09.546 align:middle
And I guess I'm just
curious what the onus is

13:09.546 --> 13:10.766 align:middle
for doing that in this cycle.

13:10.766 --> 13:13.066 align:middle
What would make you guys
decide it's appropriate

13:13.336 --> 13:14.536 align:middle
to raise interest rates again?

13:15.596 --> 13:16.836 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:17.816 --> 13:22.506 align:middle
So the reason why we
raised interest rates is

13:22.536 --> 13:26.756 align:middle
because-generally is because we
see inflation as moving up-or,

13:27.056 --> 13:28.626 align:middle
in danger of moving
up significantly,

13:28.626 --> 13:29.936 align:middle
and we really don't
see that now.

13:30.476 --> 13:34.616 align:middle
Inflation moved down in the
first quarter of this year.

13:34.616 --> 13:37.296 align:middle
We thought that that
was due, to some extent,

13:37.296 --> 13:39.326 align:middle
to idiosyncratic or
transient factors.

13:39.326 --> 13:41.656 align:middle
That turns out to have been the
case, so it's moved back up.

13:41.656 --> 13:45.406 align:middle
But it seems to be
settling in below 2 percent.

13:45.406 --> 13:47.546 align:middle
So we really don't
see that risk.

13:48.556 --> 13:52.186 align:middle
And inflation expectations
have also kind of moved down

13:52.186 --> 13:56.106 align:middle
and sideways-both surveys and
market based over the course

13:56.106 --> 14:00.786 align:middle
of this-of, really,
the recent months.

14:00.786 --> 14:02.166 align:middle
And, you know, we think

14:02.166 --> 14:04.206 align:middle
that inflation expectations
are very important

14:04.206 --> 14:07.716 align:middle
in driving actual inflation,
and we're strongly committed

14:07.716 --> 14:10.296 align:middle
to achieving our 2
percent inflation objective

14:10.296 --> 14:11.286 align:middle
on a symmetric basis.

14:11.286 --> 14:12.956 align:middle
We think it's essential
that we do that.

14:13.416 --> 14:15.706 align:middle
So we're not thinking about
raising rates right now.

14:15.846 --> 14:17.716 align:middle
There obviously will
be times in the future

14:17.716 --> 14:18.886 align:middle
where that will be appropriate.

14:19.376 --> 14:23.856 align:middle
What we're really thinking
now is that our current stance

14:23.856 --> 14:27.286 align:middle
of policy is appropriate
and will remain so as long

14:27.286 --> 14:29.896 align:middle
as the outlook is broadly in
keeping with our expectations.

14:30.326 --> 14:33.816 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

14:33.816 --> 14:35.036 align:middle
Mr.-Steve Liesman, CNBC.

14:35.036 --> 14:37.806 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, I'm-not
to make light of it,

14:37.806 --> 14:40.186 align:middle
but I'm a little bit-I
wonder if you could help me

14:40.186 --> 14:42.076 align:middle
out with the appropriate
understanding

14:42.076 --> 14:43.246 align:middle
of the word "appropriate" here.

14:44.736 --> 14:48.386 align:middle
So the statement says that
the Committee is going

14:48.386 --> 14:50.556 align:middle
"to monitor the implications
of incoming information

14:50.556 --> 14:53.356 align:middle
for the outlook as it
assesses the appropriate path

14:53.356 --> 14:54.236 align:middle
of the target range."

14:54.756 --> 14:56.846 align:middle
That tells me you
could go either way.

14:57.586 --> 14:59.886 align:middle
You came in, and you used
the word "appropriate,"

15:00.256 --> 15:02.866 align:middle
that the current rate is
"likely to remain appropriate,"

15:03.206 --> 15:04.386 align:middle
which means you're on hold.

15:05.156 --> 15:07.826 align:middle
So how do I walk away from this,
and what should we walk away

15:07.826 --> 15:09.926 align:middle
from this believing what
"appropriate" means?

15:09.926 --> 15:12.416 align:middle
Are you on hold and
need to be proved wrong

15:12.586 --> 15:14.926 align:middle
that you should remain on
hold, or is it, really,

15:14.926 --> 15:15.926 align:middle
you could go either way here?

15:16.076 --> 15:16.406 align:middle
Thank you.

15:16.406 --> 15:16.616 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:16.736 --> 15:19.766 align:middle
So we think that the current
stance of policy is likely

15:19.766 --> 15:23.386 align:middle
to remain appropriate-likely
to remain appropriate-as long

15:23.386 --> 15:24.466 align:middle
as incoming information

15:24.466 --> 15:27.406 align:middle
about the economy is broadly
consistent with our outlook,

15:27.726 --> 15:30.356 align:middle
which is a positive one of
moderate economic growth,

15:31.016 --> 15:32.196 align:middle
strong labor market,

15:32.366 --> 15:34.676 align:middle
and inflation moving
close to 2 percent.

15:34.806 --> 15:36.206 align:middle
That's-so that's what
we're saying about that.

15:36.496 --> 15:39.316 align:middle
We also say, of course,
if developments emerge

15:39.316 --> 15:42.226 align:middle
that cause a material
reassessment of that outlook,

15:42.736 --> 15:43.866 align:middle
we would respond accordingly.

15:44.016 --> 15:45.496 align:middle
So that's really how
we're thinking about it.

15:45.596 --> 15:48.916 align:middle
I can't point to one
data point or one thing

15:48.916 --> 15:50.036 align:middle
that would change our minds.

15:50.036 --> 15:53.306 align:middle
It really-it would be a
reassessment of-a material

15:53.306 --> 15:54.876 align:middle
reassessment of our
overall outlook,

15:54.876 --> 15:56.056 align:middle
which is what I described.

15:56.056 --> 15:56.736 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

15:56.856 --> 15:59.326 align:middle
I'm sorry, just to-why
isn't the phrase "likely

15:59.326 --> 16:00.656 align:middle
to remain appropriate"
in the statement

16:00.656 --> 16:02.036 align:middle
if that's the sense
of the Committee?

16:03.606 --> 16:04.656 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:04.846 --> 16:08.686 align:middle
Well, there's-there's a lot of,
you know, practice and science

16:08.686 --> 16:10.286 align:middle
and history, in terms
of how much you put

16:10.286 --> 16:12.836 align:middle
in the actual postmeeting
statement as opposed

16:12.836 --> 16:14.636 align:middle
to what we say in the
press conference statement.

16:14.636 --> 16:17.006 align:middle
It's a-you know,
it's a judgment call.

16:17.066 --> 16:20.286 align:middle
You know, I'm saying it
now, so- STEVE LIESMAN.

16:21.296 --> 16:23.406 align:middle
Thanks. NICK TIMIRAOS.

16:23.906 --> 16:24.406 align:middle
Thank you.

16:24.406 --> 16:25.976 align:middle
Nick Timiraos with the
Wall Street Journal.

16:26.496 --> 16:29.566 align:middle
You described the recent
slide, Chair Powell,

16:29.566 --> 16:31.816 align:middle
in inflation expectations
as "unwelcome."

16:31.816 --> 16:33.636 align:middle
You said that inflation
expectations are

16:33.636 --> 16:34.356 align:middle
"very important."

16:34.696 --> 16:38.026 align:middle
What, if anything, would the
Committee be prepared to do

16:38.026 --> 16:39.376 align:middle
to address this slide

16:39.376 --> 16:42.476 align:middle
in inflation expectations
if it continued?

16:42.476 --> 16:43.896 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:44.156 --> 16:45.466 align:middle
So, as I mentioned, we do think

16:45.466 --> 16:48.156 align:middle
that inflation expectations
are-they're quite essential,

16:48.156 --> 16:51.106 align:middle
quite central in our framework
of how we think about inflation.

16:51.106 --> 16:52.046 align:middle
We need them to be anchored

16:52.046 --> 16:55.116 align:middle
in a level-at a level
that's consistent

16:55.116 --> 16:57.006 align:middle
with our symmetric 2
percent inflation goal.

16:57.756 --> 17:04.936 align:middle
And we think that we need
to conduct policy in a way

17:05.336 --> 17:06.596 align:middle
that supports that outcome.

17:06.766 --> 17:07.976 align:middle
That's what we're doing now.

17:08.756 --> 17:10.946 align:middle
We're also, as part
of our review,

17:11.326 --> 17:15.266 align:middle
looking at potential
innovations,

17:15.266 --> 17:17.416 align:middle
changes to the way we
think about things,

17:17.416 --> 17:18.426 align:middle
changes to the framework

17:19.016 --> 17:22.726 align:middle
that would lead us-that
would be more supportive

17:22.726 --> 17:23.936 align:middle
of achieving inflation

17:23.936 --> 17:27.326 align:middle
on a 2 percent-on a symmetric
2 percent basis over time.

17:27.696 --> 17:29.806 align:middle
That's at the very heart of
what we're doing in the review.

17:29.806 --> 17:31.546 align:middle
It's too early to be
announcing decisions.

17:31.546 --> 17:32.406 align:middle
We haven't made them yet.

17:32.406 --> 17:35.186 align:middle
But we're in the middle
of thinking about ways

17:35.186 --> 17:38.866 align:middle
that we can make that symmetric
2 percent inflation objective

17:38.866 --> 17:42.886 align:middle
more credible by achieving
symmetric 2 percent inflation.

17:42.886 --> 17:45.386 align:middle
And it comes down to
using your policy tools

17:45.386 --> 17:47.456 align:middle
to achieve 2 percent inflation,

17:47.596 --> 17:51.906 align:middle
and that is the-that is
the thing that must happen

17:51.906 --> 17:53.176 align:middle
for credibility in this area.

17:53.176 --> 17:54.286 align:middle
So we're committed
to doing that.

17:54.436 --> 17:55.106 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

17:55.106 --> 17:55.676 align:middle
How soon do you think

17:55.676 --> 17:57.246 align:middle
that review might be
announced to the public?

17:57.246 --> 17:57.676 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:57.676 --> 17:59.926 align:middle
So we're in the middle-we're
really quite in the middle

17:59.926 --> 18:04.456 align:middle
of it now, and my thinking
is still that it will run

18:05.746 --> 18:06.936 align:middle
into the middle of next year.

18:07.126 --> 18:08.506 align:middle
These are-you know,
these changes

18:08.506 --> 18:11.676 align:middle
to monetary policy frameworks
happen-they don't happen really

18:11.676 --> 18:12.646 align:middle
quickly, let's say.

18:12.746 --> 18:15.586 align:middle
Inflation targeting took
many years to evolve.

18:15.586 --> 18:16.936 align:middle
I don't think we'll
take many years here.

18:16.936 --> 18:20.596 align:middle
I think we'll wrap it
up around the middle

18:20.596 --> 18:21.926 align:middle
of next year, would be my guess.

18:21.926 --> 18:26.246 align:middle
I have some confidence in that.

18:27.006 --> 18:28.196 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

18:28.196 --> 18:29.666 align:middle
Edward Lawrence of Fox Business.

18:29.666 --> 18:30.946 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman,
for taking this.

18:31.286 --> 18:34.766 align:middle
So how big of a change, then, to
monetary policy would there be

18:34.766 --> 18:36.356 align:middle
if you get some of that
uncertainty cleared

18:36.356 --> 18:40.046 align:middle
up-specifically, the phase-one
China trade deal finished

18:40.046 --> 18:41.456 align:middle
and USMCA ratified?

18:41.736 --> 18:45.436 align:middle
How big of a change could
that be to the monetary policy

18:45.436 --> 18:48.816 align:middle
as it stands here, and could
we see rate cuts next year-or,

18:48.816 --> 18:49.886 align:middle
rate hikes next year?

18:49.886 --> 18:50.146 align:middle
I'm sorry.

18:50.516 --> 18:50.936 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:50.936 --> 18:52.006 align:middle
Rate hikes for next year.

18:52.006 --> 18:52.746 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

18:52.746 --> 18:54.056 align:middle
Yes, because I've
heard the argument

18:54.056 --> 18:55.936 align:middle
that trade's causing
the uncertainty.

18:55.936 --> 18:57.396 align:middle
Uncertainty is keeping
the prices low.

18:57.946 --> 19:00.336 align:middle
I've heard that argument
from a Fed president

19:00.676 --> 19:01.796 align:middle
from one of the Districts.

19:02.016 --> 19:04.216 align:middle
So you remove that
uncertainty, prices,

19:04.216 --> 19:05.176 align:middle
then, would naturally rise.

19:05.176 --> 19:08.326 align:middle
So could there be a point, or
how-I guess my question is,

19:08.326 --> 19:12.386 align:middle
how big of an impact on
monetary policy would removing

19:12.386 --> 19:13.406 align:middle
that uncertainty be?

19:13.406 --> 19:14.056 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:14.056 --> 19:15.716 align:middle
Yes. So I would just
say that a-if we were

19:15.716 --> 19:19.646 align:middle
to have a sustained reduction in
trade tensions-broad reduction

19:19.646 --> 19:21.136 align:middle
in trade tensions
and a resolution

19:21.136 --> 19:24.216 align:middle
of these uncertainties,
that would bode well

19:24.216 --> 19:25.966 align:middle
for business sentiment,

19:26.256 --> 19:28.546 align:middle
which is-trade uncertainty's
been weighing

19:29.746 --> 19:31.486 align:middle
on business sentiment,
in our judgment,

19:31.486 --> 19:33.896 align:middle
in the judgment of
many analysts.

19:34.406 --> 19:36.306 align:middle
And, ultimately, it
could affect activity.

19:36.306 --> 19:38.936 align:middle
I wouldn't expect that
the effects on activity

19:38.936 --> 19:40.776 align:middle
or confidence would
be immediate,

19:40.776 --> 19:44.606 align:middle
or-would there be immediate
effects in economic activity.

19:44.606 --> 19:50.446 align:middle
I think it would take some
time after the recent things,

19:50.446 --> 19:52.396 align:middle
but I do think it would be
quite positive over time.

19:52.726 --> 19:54.206 align:middle
You come back to the
question of raising rates.

19:54.206 --> 19:55.646 align:middle
So that's really
about inflation.

19:56.106 --> 20:02.526 align:middle
And you know, we haven't yet-we
just touched 2 percent core

20:02.526 --> 20:05.826 align:middle
inflation, to pick one
measure-just touched it

20:05.826 --> 20:08.566 align:middle
for a few months, and
then we've fallen back.

20:08.566 --> 20:12.846 align:middle
So I think we would need to
see a really significant move

20:12.846 --> 20:15.266 align:middle
up in inflation that's
persistent before we would

20:15.266 --> 20:17.516 align:middle
consider raising rates to
address inflation concerns.

20:19.346 --> 20:22.516 align:middle
BRENDAN GREELEY.

20:23.236 --> 20:24.806 align:middle
Brendan Greeley,
the Financial Times.

20:24.806 --> 20:26.486 align:middle
The other thing you've talked

20:26.486 --> 20:29.876 align:middle
about in this review
is the gains

20:29.946 --> 20:34.126 align:middle
that a super high-pressure
labor market have, particularly

20:34.126 --> 20:35.676 align:middle
for the communities that
you're talking about.

20:35.676 --> 20:37.466 align:middle
You mentioned it just now
when you were talking.

20:37.466 --> 20:39.486 align:middle
If that's something
that you're identifying,

20:39.486 --> 20:41.056 align:middle
and that's something that
the Fed is learning more

20:41.056 --> 20:45.396 align:middle
about this year, why not
continue to push those gains,

20:45.716 --> 20:48.306 align:middle
particularly for people who
are reentering the labor force?

20:48.616 --> 20:49.426 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:49.676 --> 20:51.076 align:middle
Well, I think we're doing that.

20:51.076 --> 20:55.826 align:middle
I mean, I think we're
keeping-I think we've made very

20:55.826 --> 20:58.746 align:middle
substantial adjustments
to policy over the course

20:58.746 --> 20:59.886 align:middle
of this year, if
you think about it.

20:59.936 --> 21:02.976 align:middle
We entered the year expecting
some further rate increases,

21:03.226 --> 21:05.596 align:middle
we went to "patient," now
we've done three rate cuts.

21:05.596 --> 21:09.106 align:middle
It's a very substantial
shift, and the effects

21:09.106 --> 21:10.366 align:middle
of it will be felt over time.

21:10.366 --> 21:14.576 align:middle
So we feel like those
shifts are appropriate

21:14.576 --> 21:16.876 align:middle
to support exactly the
outcomes you're talking about,

21:17.266 --> 21:19.556 align:middle
which are a continuing
strong labor market,

21:19.556 --> 21:21.016 align:middle
continued strong job creation.

21:21.116 --> 21:22.026 align:middle
BRENDAN GREELEY.

21:23.076 --> 21:25.586 align:middle
So if you were to describe the
shifts that you've made so far,

21:25.586 --> 21:27.126 align:middle
it wasn't just about
trade uncertainty.

21:27.126 --> 21:28.446 align:middle
It was about, specifically,

21:28.686 --> 21:30.086 align:middle
these kinds of high-pressure
labor gains

21:30.086 --> 21:30.616 align:middle
that you've talked about.

21:30.616 --> 21:30.826 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

21:30.826 --> 21:33.056 align:middle
Well, no. It was-so we've said
it was about three things.

21:33.096 --> 21:35.986 align:middle
It's been about the
slowing global economy.

21:35.986 --> 21:37.676 align:middle
You have a synchronized slowdown

21:37.676 --> 21:39.406 align:middle
in economic activity
around the globe.

21:39.786 --> 21:42.606 align:middle
It's been building for
just about 18 months now,

21:42.686 --> 21:44.526 align:middle
and that's having an
effect on U.S. activity.

21:44.526 --> 21:48.346 align:middle
That's part of the weakness
in manufacturing, export,

21:48.346 --> 21:49.406 align:middle
and business investment.

21:49.896 --> 21:51.636 align:middle
We've had trade policy
uncertainty,

21:51.636 --> 21:54.156 align:middle
which we think also has
been weighing on activity

21:54.246 --> 21:56.446 align:middle
and investment and sentiment.

21:56.636 --> 21:59.736 align:middle
And we've had inflation,
which-we've called out the risk

21:59.926 --> 22:03.346 align:middle
of inflation running
persistently below 2 percent

22:03.916 --> 22:05.706 align:middle
as a risk that we
needed to address.

22:05.926 --> 22:08.016 align:middle
So we've been doing it to
address all of those things.

22:08.496 --> 22:10.486 align:middle
I would say, the gains

22:10.486 --> 22:14.436 align:middle
in the labor market
have been great to see.

22:14.756 --> 22:18.886 align:middle
It's particularly the fact
that people at the lower end

22:18.886 --> 22:21.736 align:middle
of wages have been getting
most of the benefit of most

22:21.736 --> 22:23.466 align:middle
of the wage gains in the
last couple of years.

22:23.466 --> 22:24.286 align:middle
That's a great thing.

22:24.286 --> 22:28.096 align:middle
You know from our Fed Listens
events that we've been hearing

22:28.096 --> 22:29.886 align:middle
from people who live
and work in low-

22:29.886 --> 22:31.066 align:middle
and moderate-income communities

22:31.066 --> 22:33.786 align:middle
that this is the best
labor market they've seen

22:33.786 --> 22:35.416 align:middle
in their lifetime,
things like that.

22:35.896 --> 22:38.076 align:middle
And, by the way, that
there's also-there's still

22:38.076 --> 22:40.516 align:middle
so many people who
are, you know,

22:40.516 --> 22:42.506 align:middle
still not in the
labor market yet,

22:42.506 --> 22:45.446 align:middle
and that-there's just a lot more
good that can be done there.

22:45.716 --> 22:47.796 align:middle
At the same time, we have to
think about the whole economy,

22:48.626 --> 22:51.136 align:middle
and-but, yes, those
gains are very positive.

22:51.136 --> 22:52.046 align:middle
We do call them out.

22:52.046 --> 22:54.576 align:middle
They are a reason for us to
want to extend the expansion,

22:54.576 --> 22:57.536 align:middle
and that's something
we're committed to doing.

22:57.536 --> 22:58.806 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

23:00.286 --> 23:07.956 align:middle
On the Fed's balance sheet-you
all recently resumed purchases

23:07.956 --> 23:10.916 align:middle
of Treasury bills, and
I was just wondering,

23:10.916 --> 23:15.026 align:middle
how long do you expect
that to continue?

23:15.316 --> 23:17.656 align:middle
And, given the fact
that, you know,

23:17.686 --> 23:20.316 align:middle
the repo operations-it
seems like the Fed is having

23:20.316 --> 23:22.786 align:middle
to increase the amount of
liquidity-temporary liquidity

23:22.786 --> 23:25.006 align:middle
that it's injecting
into the system.

23:25.006 --> 23:26.346 align:middle
You know, do you still feel

23:26.346 --> 23:30.896 align:middle
like it's just-that there is not
enough reserves in the system?

23:30.896 --> 23:33.206 align:middle
Do you feel like you have a good
sense of what's going on there?

23:33.276 --> 23:34.046 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:34.046 --> 23:36.316 align:middle
Okay. So, on the "how
long," what we've said is

23:36.316 --> 23:39.726 align:middle
that we expect bill
purchases to continue at least

23:39.726 --> 23:41.516 align:middle
into the second quarter
of next year.

23:41.836 --> 23:44.346 align:middle
And we've said that temporary
open market operations we expect

23:44.346 --> 23:47.606 align:middle
to continue, I think,
at least until the end

23:47.606 --> 23:48.886 align:middle
of January, I believe.

23:49.596 --> 23:50.626 align:middle
Yes, through January.

23:50.726 --> 23:54.396 align:middle
In terms of the causes,
so there's a lot

23:54.396 --> 23:57.556 align:middle
of forensic work going on by
us and by market participants

23:57.556 --> 23:59.586 align:middle
and all kinds of analysts.

23:59.636 --> 24:05.576 align:middle
And, you know, one thing is
that we think we need reserves

24:05.576 --> 24:08.936 align:middle
to be back up to the level-the
minimum level of reserves

24:08.936 --> 24:11.996 align:middle
that we can have during
the various fluctuations

24:11.996 --> 24:17.186 align:middle
that you see with reserves is
something like $1.45 trillion

24:17.476 --> 24:20.686 align:middle
or a little higher, and that's
the level in early September.

24:20.736 --> 24:21.986 align:middle
So we're going to
be adding reserves

24:21.986 --> 24:23.696 align:middle
to get back to that place.

24:23.696 --> 24:24.596 align:middle
That's one thing.

24:25.036 --> 24:29.596 align:middle
There are also-it may be-one
thing that was surprising

24:29.596 --> 24:33.166 align:middle
about the episode was that
liquidity didn't seem to flow

24:34.066 --> 24:35.306 align:middle
as one might have expected.

24:35.306 --> 24:37.796 align:middle
We had surveyed the
banks carefully

24:38.036 --> 24:41.416 align:middle
about what was their lowest
comfortable level of reserves,

24:41.806 --> 24:46.406 align:middle
and many banks that were well
above that level did not take

24:46.406 --> 24:48.126 align:middle
that excess cash and invest it

24:48.206 --> 24:51.296 align:middle
in the repo market
at much higher rates.

24:51.296 --> 24:55.236 align:middle
They just-they didn't do that,
and so the question is why.

24:55.236 --> 24:59.306 align:middle
And are there things that we
can do that would-adjustments

24:59.306 --> 25:02.186 align:middle
that we could make that
would allow liquidity

25:02.186 --> 25:04.136 align:middle
to flow more easily
in the system

25:04.196 --> 25:07.296 align:middle
without in any way
sacrificing safety and soundness

25:07.296 --> 25:08.306 align:middle
or financial stability?

25:08.566 --> 25:09.486 align:middle
So we're looking at those.

25:09.486 --> 25:10.936 align:middle
Those are not things
that can happen

25:11.226 --> 25:14.906 align:middle
that can really address the
situation in the short term,

25:14.906 --> 25:17.496 align:middle
but those are a range of things
that we're looking at as well.

25:19.276 --> 25:20.126 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

25:21.326 --> 25:25.466 align:middle
Howard Schneider with Reuters,
and thanks for the questions.

25:25.756 --> 25:28.396 align:middle
I wonder if you could give us a
little more texture on, sort of,

25:28.396 --> 25:31.216 align:middle
what the strongest or most
important case is for feeling

25:31.216 --> 25:33.306 align:middle
that you've now taken
out adequate insurance.

25:33.386 --> 25:36.756 align:middle
Is it the response you're seeing
in housing and other parts

25:36.756 --> 25:40.106 align:middle
of the economy, arguably,
to the cuts made so far?

25:40.776 --> 25:43.026 align:middle
Is it the fact that
the risk environment

25:43.026 --> 25:46.526 align:middle
out there globally has
shifted a bit and abated a bit?

25:47.136 --> 25:49.546 align:middle
Or is it something
different, you know,

25:49.546 --> 25:50.566 align:middle
in the financial markets,

25:50.606 --> 25:53.836 align:middle
the fact that the yield curve
has righted itself, et cetera?

25:55.106 --> 25:55.426 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:55.426 --> 25:58.606 align:middle
Honestly, it's really
the-there isn't any one factor.

25:58.746 --> 26:00.866 align:middle
It's really the whole
range of economic data

26:00.866 --> 26:06.636 align:middle
and also what we see
as-about our stance of policy,

26:06.636 --> 26:09.126 align:middle
which we believe is
the appropriate stance.

26:09.126 --> 26:10.126 align:middle
It's really both
of those things.

26:10.126 --> 26:12.896 align:middle
Again, if you look at the
performance of the economy

26:12.896 --> 26:15.396 align:middle
through this, the consumer
sector has been quite resilient.

26:15.876 --> 26:17.906 align:middle
Witness today's GDP report.

26:18.666 --> 26:21.926 align:middle
And it's just-again,
all the things I went

26:21.926 --> 26:22.796 align:middle
through earlier suggest

26:22.796 --> 26:24.296 align:middle
that that sector
continues to be strong.

26:24.486 --> 26:27.496 align:middle
We know that the manufacturing,
investment, trade,

26:27.496 --> 26:29.606 align:middle
export sector has been weak.

26:29.606 --> 26:32.336 align:middle
That continues, but nonetheless,
overall, we've got an economy

26:32.846 --> 26:36.706 align:middle
that is showing moderate
growth, and we think

26:36.706 --> 26:40.406 align:middle
that today's setting-we're
now at 1.5 to 1.75 percent.

26:40.986 --> 26:44.736 align:middle
That is, we believe, an
accommodative level and a level

26:44.736 --> 26:46.886 align:middle
that will support that
outlook that we have.

26:46.996 --> 26:47.416 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

26:47.536 --> 26:49.686 align:middle
But are you concerned-just
to follow up, really.

26:49.826 --> 26:52.306 align:middle
This is kind of a
one-legged recovery,

26:52.306 --> 26:56.396 align:middle
that unless you get some impetus
from other things-exports,

26:57.176 --> 27:01.286 align:middle
investment-that it's going to
weaken further at some point?

27:01.286 --> 27:02.006 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:02.006 --> 27:03.566 align:middle
You know, we don't see
any evidence of that.

27:03.566 --> 27:06.966 align:middle
What we see is-what we see is

27:07.226 --> 27:09.176 align:middle
that the consumer
sector continues strong.

27:09.376 --> 27:11.056 align:middle
Again, low initial claims.

27:11.056 --> 27:13.476 align:middle
It's all-the data we
monitor, obviously,

27:13.476 --> 27:14.806 align:middle
is a very broad range of data.

27:15.186 --> 27:20.556 align:middle
We also see now, I think,
more clearly the effects

27:20.556 --> 27:23.366 align:middle
of more accommodative
monetary policy

27:23.366 --> 27:25.756 align:middle
on various kinds of
consumer activity.

27:26.196 --> 27:29.926 align:middle
You're seeing strong
durable goods sales.

27:30.036 --> 27:32.176 align:middle
You're seeing housing now
contributing to growth

27:32.176 --> 27:34.196 align:middle
for the first time in
a while-two-tenths,

27:34.196 --> 27:35.106 align:middle
I think, this quarter.

27:35.826 --> 27:41.336 align:middle
And you're seeing retail
sales-the retail sales number,

27:41.666 --> 27:43.526 align:middle
the PCE number, as I mentioned,

27:44.046 --> 27:46.346 align:middle
in today's release
was 2.9 percent.

27:46.346 --> 27:46.906 align:middle
So that's good.

27:46.906 --> 27:48.706 align:middle
And I think, more broadly,

27:48.786 --> 27:51.886 align:middle
monetary policy is also
supporting household spending

27:51.886 --> 27:54.046 align:middle
and home buying by keeping
the labor market strong,

27:54.046 --> 27:57.536 align:middle
keeping workers' incomes rising,
and keeping consumer confidence

27:57.536 --> 28:01.126 align:middle
at high levels, where
it currently is.

28:01.266 --> 28:02.276 align:middle
SCOTT HORSLEY.

28:02.436 --> 28:09.596 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

28:10.016 --> 28:12.226 align:middle
You were just talking about how
the lower rates have affected

28:12.256 --> 28:13.186 align:middle
consumer behavior.

28:13.186 --> 28:15.516 align:middle
Is there a concern that
there's just less potency

28:15.966 --> 28:17.886 align:middle
on the business investment
side that you're,

28:17.976 --> 28:19.356 align:middle
in effect, pushing on a string?

28:19.816 --> 28:20.286 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:20.526 --> 28:23.436 align:middle
Well, I think monetary
policy works

28:23.436 --> 28:25.256 align:middle
through the channels
that we understand.

28:25.736 --> 28:28.316 align:middle
I think the effects are
clear in what we think

28:28.316 --> 28:30.856 align:middle
of as the
interest-rate-sensitive sectors,

28:30.856 --> 28:32.486 align:middle
which are the ones-some of
the ones that I mentioned.

28:32.846 --> 28:35.746 align:middle
I think it's-I think
interest rates are a factor

28:36.296 --> 28:37.886 align:middle
in business investment,

28:37.886 --> 28:41.116 align:middle
but I don't believe they're the
main factor and main driver.

28:41.776 --> 28:44.606 align:middle
And I think, you
know, what one would

28:44.606 --> 28:49.086 align:middle
like to see for-to support
greater manufacturing activity,

28:49.166 --> 28:52.196 align:middle
business investment, and exports
would be a global recovery.

28:52.716 --> 28:55.216 align:middle
And, you know, there's a lot of
monetary policy accommodation

28:55.216 --> 28:57.276 align:middle
and some fiscal accommodation,
maybe,

28:57.706 --> 28:59.466 align:middle
in the global economy now.

28:59.656 --> 29:01.946 align:middle
Not here, but perhaps elsewhere.

29:02.606 --> 29:06.336 align:middle
And that should turn-that
should support growth

29:06.336 --> 29:07.916 align:middle
in the global economy.

29:07.916 --> 29:13.096 align:middle
And, again, resolution of our
trade issues would contribute

29:13.096 --> 29:14.016 align:middle
to that over time as well.

29:15.696 --> 29:19.666 align:middle
CHRIS RUGABER.

29:19.986 --> 29:20.566 align:middle
Thank you.

29:20.566 --> 29:22.696 align:middle
Chris Rugaber at
Associated Press.

29:22.696 --> 29:24.826 align:middle
You mentioned Fed
Listens a couple of times,

29:24.826 --> 29:27.506 align:middle
and what you've heard
from people there

29:27.506 --> 29:29.576 align:middle
about how it's benefited
the disadvantaged

29:29.576 --> 29:32.476 align:middle
or lower-income workers-the
hot job market we have.

29:33.006 --> 29:35.296 align:middle
Has the Fed thought about
institutionalizing something

29:35.296 --> 29:38.496 align:middle
like Fed Listens and maybe
taking in some of this input

29:38.496 --> 29:41.066 align:middle
from people that you don't
always hear from-things

29:41.066 --> 29:43.726 align:middle
like the Beige Book
focus on businesses?

29:43.726 --> 29:46.136 align:middle
Has there been thought of
bringing in folks, say,

29:46.136 --> 29:48.356 align:middle
from community groups
more regularly?

29:48.856 --> 29:49.126 align:middle
Thank you.

29:49.456 --> 29:49.736 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:50.166 --> 29:53.416 align:middle
We think-we think that Fed
Listens has been a great success

29:53.416 --> 29:56.056 align:middle
for us, and I'm sure
we will repeat it.

29:56.056 --> 29:57.676 align:middle
In fact, I would imagine

29:57.676 --> 30:00.806 align:middle
that this entire monetary
policy review will become

30:00.806 --> 30:03.876 align:middle
institutionalized and
be done every few years.

30:04.366 --> 30:07.786 align:middle
I would say this:
In the Beige Book,

30:07.786 --> 30:10.646 align:middle
we talk to educational
institutions,

30:10.646 --> 30:12.826 align:middle
health-care institutions,
community groups,

30:12.826 --> 30:14.676 align:middle
labor groups-it's
not just businesses.

30:15.046 --> 30:17.046 align:middle
And, by the way, all of those
groups are also represented

30:17.046 --> 30:19.126 align:middle
on the boards of
our regional banks.

30:19.536 --> 30:23.566 align:middle
We also meet quite regularly at
the Board with representatives

30:23.566 --> 30:25.306 align:middle
of low- and moderate-income
communities.

30:25.306 --> 30:28.256 align:middle
And so we're-we're very
conscious of-you know,

30:28.256 --> 30:30.546 align:middle
that we represent all Americans,
and we need to hear all

30:30.546 --> 30:31.406 align:middle
of their perspectives.

30:31.406 --> 30:33.596 align:middle
That's why we-you know, we talk

30:33.596 --> 30:35.276 align:middle
about how low the
unemployment rate is,

30:35.546 --> 30:37.846 align:middle
the aggregate unemployment,
but we also talk about groups

30:37.846 --> 30:39.136 align:middle
that haven't experienced
that yet.

30:39.246 --> 30:43.086 align:middle
And so we're-we try
to remind ourselves

30:43.346 --> 30:45.066 align:middle
that we serve everybody.

30:46.206 --> 30:48.836 align:middle
STEVE MATTHEWS.

30:49.816 --> 30:51.406 align:middle
Steve Matthews with Bloomberg.

30:52.256 --> 30:56.166 align:middle
You mentioned housing
and today's GDP data.

30:56.776 --> 30:59.346 align:middle
When you look at
that, do you believe

30:59.346 --> 31:01.666 align:middle
that you have achieved
a soft landing

31:01.666 --> 31:04.806 align:middle
or are achieving a soft landing,
in the sense that we're moving

31:04.806 --> 31:07.556 align:middle
to trend growth for the rest
of the year and next year?

31:07.926 --> 31:08.436 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:09.186 --> 31:12.846 align:middle
So that is-our outlook
overall is for moderate growth

31:13.016 --> 31:15.306 align:middle
of around 2 percent, which
is pretty close to trend.

31:15.346 --> 31:17.856 align:middle
That's our outlook: could
be better, could be worse.

31:17.856 --> 31:20.816 align:middle
You know, it's-you never
say you've achieved it.

31:20.816 --> 31:23.036 align:middle
But that's our outlook.

31:23.036 --> 31:27.686 align:middle
And we feel like our current
stance of policy is appropriate

31:28.056 --> 31:30.406 align:middle
as long as that remains
broadly our outlook.

31:31.086 --> 31:32.786 align:middle
DONNA BORAK.

31:35.846 --> 31:37.156 align:middle
Donna Borak with CNN.

31:37.526 --> 31:40.416 align:middle
Chair Powell, setting
aside market expectations

31:40.416 --> 31:44.196 align:middle
for today's rate cut-obviously,
97 percent sort of expected

31:44.196 --> 31:47.026 align:middle
that this would happen today-can
you tell us a little bit more

31:47.026 --> 31:49.276 align:middle
about the rationale
behind moving

31:49.336 --> 31:52.076 align:middle
at this particular meeting as
opposed to waiting six weeks

31:52.076 --> 31:54.906 align:middle
and cutting in December, and
if there was any discussion

31:54.906 --> 31:56.106 align:middle
about that around the table?

31:56.226 --> 31:57.876 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

31:57.946 --> 32:04.686 align:middle
Yes. I think this seems
to be the right movement.

32:04.686 --> 32:07.126 align:middle
You can see that we
had our usual range

32:07.126 --> 32:08.526 align:middle
of perspectives, range of views.

32:08.626 --> 32:11.236 align:middle
But, in the end, we had a strong
vote in favor of this action.

32:12.066 --> 32:14.336 align:middle
I strongly believe
it's the right action.

32:14.336 --> 32:16.536 align:middle
I strongly believe that
the actions we've taken

32:16.536 --> 32:19.266 align:middle
over the course of this year
have been the right things to do

32:19.266 --> 32:22.436 align:middle
for this economy and are
supporting economic growth

32:22.436 --> 32:23.516 align:middle
and will do so in the future.

32:28.536 --> 32:29.636 align:middle
So- GREG ROBB.

32:31.876 --> 32:32.526 align:middle
Thank you.

32:32.616 --> 32:34.216 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

32:34.886 --> 32:38.566 align:middle
Chairman Powell, it seems
that the differences all

32:38.566 --> 32:41.396 align:middle
across the country-that
different regions are reacting

32:41.396 --> 32:43.526 align:middle
differently to the China trade,

32:43.526 --> 32:46.026 align:middle
and some regions are
much weaker than others.

32:46.026 --> 32:48.336 align:middle
There's this urban
versus rural divide,

32:48.716 --> 32:50.846 align:middle
the coasts versus the
middle of the country.

32:51.366 --> 32:55.416 align:middle
How does that factor into
your policy decisions?

32:55.416 --> 32:55.626 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:55.916 --> 32:59.136 align:middle
You know, it's-monetary policy
is famously a blunt instrument.

32:59.136 --> 33:02.296 align:middle
So we can't-obviously, we can't
raise interest rates some place

33:02.296 --> 33:03.436 align:middle
and lower them other places.

33:03.966 --> 33:05.546 align:middle
But we're very conscious.

33:05.586 --> 33:07.776 align:middle
The first thing to know
is to understand that,

33:07.776 --> 33:11.756 align:middle
that-and we've called that
out, as you, I'm sure, know,

33:11.756 --> 33:15.156 align:middle
in one of our recent Monetary
Policy Reports to Congress.

33:15.156 --> 33:19.426 align:middle
The sort of rural-urban
disparities in-you know,

33:19.426 --> 33:21.326 align:middle
in employment, in
growth, in all kinds

33:21.326 --> 33:22.706 align:middle
of things, in health outcomes.

33:22.816 --> 33:27.526 align:middle
You know, it's a big and growing
difference-set of differences.

33:27.936 --> 33:30.336 align:middle
We don't really have, you know,
the tools to address that,

33:30.406 --> 33:34.076 align:middle
but we call things out that
are important to our economy

33:34.226 --> 33:37.456 align:middle
that we may not be-that we might
have-may not have the right

33:37.456 --> 33:40.426 align:middle
tools to best address them,
but we call them out because,

33:40.426 --> 33:42.736 align:middle
you know, we're spending all
of our time with the economy,

33:42.736 --> 33:45.656 align:middle
and those are issues that
maybe have to be addressed

33:45.656 --> 33:49.906 align:middle
by legislatures and, you
know, by-at the national level

33:50.116 --> 33:51.766 align:middle
and at the state level.

33:51.766 --> 33:52.046 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

33:52.046 --> 33:52.426 align:middle
Oh, could I follow?

33:52.426 --> 33:54.136 align:middle
Sorry, could I follow?

33:54.666 --> 33:55.306 align:middle
Could-sorry.

33:55.886 --> 33:58.586 align:middle
Just, so-so are you asking
Congress to do something now,

33:58.586 --> 34:03.066 align:middle
or will you ask them to do
something down the road?

34:03.066 --> 34:03.626 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

34:05.016 --> 34:06.816 align:middle
I'm not asking Congress
to do anything right now.

34:07.486 --> 34:11.556 align:middle
I sometimes have noted that
it would be appropriate.

34:11.556 --> 34:16.356 align:middle
Let me put it this way: We
do what we do, which is,

34:16.356 --> 34:18.586 align:middle
we're assigned a job,
which is maximum employment

34:18.586 --> 34:19.746 align:middle
and stable prices.

34:20.196 --> 34:21.636 align:middle
We do financial stability.

34:21.636 --> 34:22.906 align:middle
We do bank regulation.

34:23.506 --> 34:26.846 align:middle
The U.S. economy faces
significant longer-term

34:26.846 --> 34:28.596 align:middle
challenges around
potential growth,

34:28.596 --> 34:30.676 align:middle
around labor force
participation,

34:30.676 --> 34:32.986 align:middle
around disparities
of income and wealth,

34:33.576 --> 34:36.216 align:middle
around all kinds of things.

34:36.646 --> 34:38.556 align:middle
And those are issues
for Congress,

34:38.686 --> 34:39.736 align:middle
and they're not issues for us.

34:39.736 --> 34:43.306 align:middle
But if you really want the U.S.
economy to be all it can be

34:43.726 --> 34:45.716 align:middle
and raise the potential growth
rate of the United States,

34:45.716 --> 34:48.856 align:middle
you need-you need
proper monetary policy.

34:48.856 --> 34:50.296 align:middle
But, really, it isn't
monetary policy.

34:50.296 --> 34:52.506 align:middle
Really, it's fiscal
policy that supports,

34:53.566 --> 34:54.546 align:middle
you know, inclusive growth.

34:55.246 --> 35:03.846 align:middle
DON LEE. Don Lee
with the L.A. Times.

35:04.836 --> 35:07.356 align:middle
I think you said, Chair Powell,

35:07.356 --> 35:11.236 align:middle
that the primary positive
development in risks

35:11.236 --> 35:15.796 align:middle
to the outlook was on trade
and, specifically, the prospects

35:15.846 --> 35:18.966 align:middle
for a phase-one agreement
with China.

35:19.196 --> 35:23.576 align:middle
But, given the ups and downs
of the trade dispute and that,

35:23.576 --> 35:24.996 align:middle
you know, several truces

35:24.996 --> 35:29.106 align:middle
and tentative deals have fallen
through, are you concerned

35:29.106 --> 35:33.646 align:middle
about developing monetary
policy guidance, if you will,

35:33.996 --> 35:36.456 align:middle
that's dependent a
good part on the wins

35:36.456 --> 35:37.516 align:middle
of the trade negotiations?

35:37.956 --> 35:38.186 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:38.246 --> 35:40.256 align:middle
Yes, I know, that's-I
wouldn't-that's not how I would

35:40.256 --> 35:40.976 align:middle
characterize it.

35:40.976 --> 35:44.926 align:middle
I would just-I noted
that it may be

35:45.466 --> 35:51.866 align:middle
that there's been progress there
toward-away from bad outcomes

35:51.866 --> 35:54.106 align:middle
and confrontational
outcomes, and it seems

35:54.106 --> 35:57.366 align:middle
like there's the makings of
a possible settlement there.

35:57.366 --> 35:59.366 align:middle
I didn't say we're baking
that in or anything,

35:59.366 --> 36:00.756 align:middle
but I'm just noting
that that's the case.

36:00.916 --> 36:03.726 align:middle
As with Brexit-it's still quite
uncertain what will be the

36:03.726 --> 36:06.286 align:middle
result of the election and what
the ultimate result will be.

36:06.286 --> 36:07.706 align:middle
But I would say that
the tail risk

36:08.276 --> 36:13.576 align:middle
of a nonnegotiated no-deal
Brexit seems to have decreased,

36:13.766 --> 36:17.246 align:middle
just as I would say
that the situation

36:17.276 --> 36:19.036 align:middle
in our trade negotiations
with China seems

36:19.036 --> 36:21.726 align:middle
to have taken a step
closer to resolution.

36:22.456 --> 36:23.146 align:middle
That's all I'm saying.

36:23.146 --> 36:25.306 align:middle
DON LEE. If I could follow up,
if the phase-one agreement,

36:25.306 --> 36:31.156 align:middle
then, doesn't work out, then
would we be back at, you know,

36:31.466 --> 36:34.976 align:middle
making, you know, monetary
policy that- CHAIR POWELL.

36:34.976 --> 36:38.856 align:middle
That's one factor among many,
many factors that factor

36:38.856 --> 36:42.026 align:middle
into our assessment
of the outlook.

36:42.846 --> 36:48.126 align:middle
And if we see-if we
have-if we-if things happen

36:48.126 --> 36:51.066 align:middle
that cause us to materially
reassess the outlook,

36:52.086 --> 36:54.256 align:middle
change our assessment of the
outlook in a material way,

36:54.576 --> 36:57.156 align:middle
then we would act accordingly.

36:58.936 --> 37:02.136 align:middle
VIRGINIE MONTET.

37:02.926 --> 37:04.986 align:middle
Virginie Montet with the
Agence France-Presse.

37:05.116 --> 37:05.526 align:middle
Thank you.

37:05.526 --> 37:08.926 align:middle
The Fed has pointed
out many times

37:08.926 --> 37:12.076 align:middle
in the past the rising
inequality

37:12.076 --> 37:14.616 align:middle
over those past decades and how

37:14.616 --> 37:18.846 align:middle
that could be a factor
behind sluggish growth.

37:18.846 --> 37:23.316 align:middle
In light of the recent outcome
of the strike at General Motors,

37:23.766 --> 37:27.636 align:middle
do you see the union
having a role to play,

37:27.686 --> 37:30.166 align:middle
helping to raise lower
and middle incomes

37:30.166 --> 37:34.536 align:middle
and promoting more shared
growth in the long run?

37:35.006 --> 37:36.146 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:36.146 --> 37:40.766 align:middle
So, not for us to say what the
appropriate labor arrangements

37:40.766 --> 37:41.176 align:middle
will be.

37:41.876 --> 37:49.546 align:middle
I will just say, on the General
Motors strike, it's-it's likely

37:49.856 --> 37:54.626 align:middle
to have taken away a couple of
tenths of growth this quarter.

37:54.786 --> 37:56.256 align:middle
That's likely to
come back, maybe,

37:56.256 --> 37:57.646 align:middle
over the first half
of next year.

37:58.496 --> 37:59.946 align:middle
And I think it's good
to see it settled.

37:59.946 --> 38:00.976 align:middle
But I'm not going to comment

38:00.976 --> 38:03.746 align:middle
on what the appropriate
labor arrangements are.

38:03.746 --> 38:06.276 align:middle
That's a little bit far
afield from our task here.

38:06.626 --> 38:08.036 align:middle
Thank you.

38:08.446 --> 38:09.766 align:middle
PAUL KIERNAN.

38:10.016 --> 38:11.986 align:middle
Thank you, Chairman.

38:11.986 --> 38:14.476 align:middle
Paul Kiernan from
Dow Jones Newswires.

38:14.876 --> 38:18.356 align:middle
[Inaudible] Oh, I was
wondering what that was.

38:18.356 --> 38:20.276 align:middle
Paul Kiernan from
Dow Jones Newswires.

38:20.276 --> 38:22.666 align:middle
Thank you-thank you, Chairman.

38:23.086 --> 38:26.556 align:middle
Wondering if you would
categorize the current stance

38:26.556 --> 38:30.276 align:middle
of monetary policy as
accommodative, or if it's just,

38:30.356 --> 38:32.546 align:middle
you know, that neutral
was lower than we thought

38:32.546 --> 38:33.706 align:middle
at this time last year.

38:33.706 --> 38:38.406 align:middle
Secondly, you know, we're
now two years removed

38:38.406 --> 38:42.356 align:middle
from the Tax Cuts and Jobs
Act, and the economy is growing

38:42.356 --> 38:44.536 align:middle
at about the same pace
it was before-2 percent.

38:45.346 --> 38:51.766 align:middle
So, given your mention of fiscal
policy and the role it plays

38:51.766 --> 38:56.756 align:middle
in supporting long-term growth,
you know, is it fair to say

38:56.756 --> 39:00.906 align:middle
that the Tax Cuts and Jobs
Act worked the way it was

39:00.906 --> 39:01.346 align:middle
supposed to?

39:01.536 --> 39:02.736 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

39:03.726 --> 39:05.196 align:middle
Sorry, say again what
your first question was?

39:05.196 --> 39:08.496 align:middle
I wrote it down, I
can't- PAUL KIERNAN.

39:08.496 --> 39:10.486 align:middle
Sorry-if policy is
accommodative right now.

39:10.686 --> 39:11.296 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:11.716 --> 39:12.456 align:middle
Ah, yes. Okay.

39:12.456 --> 39:16.266 align:middle
So if you look at where the
federal funds rate is trading,

39:16.266 --> 39:18.256 align:middle
it should be in the middle of,
or maybe the lower half of,

39:18.256 --> 39:20.446 align:middle
the range of 150 to 175.

39:21.316 --> 39:24.996 align:middle
So that means the real rate is
probably modestly below zero.

39:25.636 --> 39:27.696 align:middle
I think my own sense would be

39:27.696 --> 39:29.756 align:middle
that that's somewhat
accommodative policy.

39:30.746 --> 39:34.816 align:middle
I would say, though,
that there's a range

39:34.816 --> 39:37.306 align:middle
of plausible estimates of what
the neutral rate of interest is,

39:37.306 --> 39:40.296 align:middle
and I think many
of those who make

39:40.296 --> 39:43.456 align:middle
such estimates have moved their
estimate down over the course

39:43.456 --> 39:45.306 align:middle
of many years, and
that process continues.

39:45.346 --> 39:48.896 align:middle
But, nonetheless, that seems
to me to be very likely

39:48.896 --> 39:50.706 align:middle
to be an accommodative
stance of policy,

39:50.706 --> 39:52.956 align:middle
and an appropriate stance,
given the situation we're

39:52.956 --> 39:57.266 align:middle
in with continuing downside
risks and-that I mentioned.

39:57.796 --> 39:59.156 align:middle
So- JEAN YUNG.

40:01.496 --> 40:03.366 align:middle
Hi. Jean Yung from Market News.

40:03.366 --> 40:06.746 align:middle
I wanted to ask about
financial stability risks.

40:07.546 --> 40:11.376 align:middle
Recently, the IMF and some other
global policymakers have been

40:11.376 --> 40:13.686 align:middle
expressing concerns
over the high level

40:13.686 --> 40:15.716 align:middle
of risky corporate debt.

40:16.376 --> 40:20.366 align:middle
So as rates get lower in the
U.S. and around the world,

40:20.546 --> 40:21.486 align:middle
are you more worried

40:21.486 --> 40:24.356 align:middle
about financial stability
risks and reach-for-yield?

40:24.356 --> 40:24.816 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:24.816 --> 40:28.026 align:middle
So we monitor financial
stability risks very carefully

40:28.026 --> 40:28.806 align:middle
all of the time.

40:28.806 --> 40:31.436 align:middle
It's what we do since
the financial crisis,

40:31.436 --> 40:32.426 align:middle
as I've mentioned before.

40:32.786 --> 40:34.776 align:middle
Currently, we don't
see large imbalances.

40:34.776 --> 40:38.766 align:middle
This long expansion is
notable for the lack

40:38.946 --> 40:40.376 align:middle
of large financial imbalances

40:40.376 --> 40:43.506 align:middle
like the ones we've seen
certainly before the

40:43.506 --> 40:45.456 align:middle
crisis happened.

40:45.826 --> 40:48.316 align:middle
So we have a four-part
framework, I'll quickly mention.

40:48.396 --> 40:50.716 align:middle
The first is leverage in the
financial system, which is low

40:50.716 --> 40:51.856 align:middle
by historical standards.

40:51.856 --> 40:55.086 align:middle
The second is funding
risk, which is the risk

40:55.086 --> 40:59.766 align:middle
of runnable funding, and that
risk is also quite low for banks

40:59.766 --> 41:02.716 align:middle
but also for, you know, the
nonbanking financial sector.

41:03.226 --> 41:07.536 align:middle
If you look at asset prices,
we see some high asset prices

41:07.536 --> 41:09.646 align:middle
but not broadly across a range.

41:10.216 --> 41:13.786 align:middle
We don't see bubbles
and that kind of thing.

41:13.786 --> 41:16.416 align:middle
And that leaves the
fourth, which is leverage

41:16.416 --> 41:17.586 align:middle
in the nonfinancial sector,

41:17.586 --> 41:19.066 align:middle
and that's households
and businesses.

41:19.066 --> 41:22.566 align:middle
So with households, again,
we don't see leverage.

41:22.566 --> 41:23.836 align:middle
We see them actually getting

41:23.836 --> 41:26.146 align:middle
in very good shape
financially in the aggregate.

41:26.146 --> 41:28.246 align:middle
Obviously, plenty of
households are not

41:28.246 --> 41:30.316 align:middle
in great shape financially,
but in the aggregate,

41:30.546 --> 41:32.096 align:middle
the household sector is
in a very good place.

41:32.456 --> 41:35.266 align:middle
That leaves businesses, which
is where the issue has been.

41:35.826 --> 41:39.596 align:middle
Leverage among corporations
and other forms of business,

41:39.596 --> 41:41.486 align:middle
private businesses,
is historically high.

41:41.946 --> 41:43.386 align:middle
We've been monitoring
it carefully

41:43.386 --> 41:44.796 align:middle
and taking appropriate steps.

41:45.836 --> 41:50.936 align:middle
So that's what I would say, but
it's-corporate debt is one part

41:51.526 --> 41:56.046 align:middle
of a larger part of our
framework, and it is something

41:56.046 --> 41:57.966 align:middle
that we're paying quite
a bit of attention to.

41:58.886 --> 42:01.306 align:middle
And it's been part
of the last couple

42:01.396 --> 42:03.046 align:middle
of Shared National Credit exams,

42:03.556 --> 42:05.106 align:middle
and we've been monitoring
it carefully

42:05.106 --> 42:06.486 align:middle
and taking appropriate action.

42:07.286 --> 42:11.146 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

42:11.846 --> 42:14.466 align:middle
Hi. Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

42:14.656 --> 42:17.726 align:middle
Have the problems in the
repo market led you to look

42:17.726 --> 42:21.896 align:middle
into whether the liquidity
and capital requirements

42:21.936 --> 42:23.846 align:middle
for banks are too high?

42:24.706 --> 42:27.626 align:middle
Is that something that
the Fed might review?

42:27.866 --> 42:28.216 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:28.956 --> 42:33.146 align:middle
So we're-I think, again, I think
the most important basic thing

42:33.146 --> 42:38.066 align:middle
is to get the level of reserves
back up so that-reserves move up

42:38.066 --> 42:39.446 align:middle
and down with some volatility.

42:39.646 --> 42:42.066 align:middle
We don't want them to move
below the level they were

42:42.066 --> 42:45.076 align:middle
at in the beginning of
September, which is, again,

42:45.406 --> 42:48.456 align:middle
between $1.45 and $1.5 trillion.

42:48.456 --> 42:50.246 align:middle
That's the main thing,
that's the first thing.

42:50.246 --> 42:51.656 align:middle
And we're on a path to do that,

42:52.146 --> 42:54.356 align:middle
between our temporary
open market operations

42:54.766 --> 42:57.096 align:middle
and also our bill purchases.

42:57.666 --> 42:59.506 align:middle
In addition to that-in
addition to that,

42:59.556 --> 43:04.316 align:middle
we're looking at-there are-it's
a big, complicated marketplace,

43:04.316 --> 43:08.166 align:middle
and there was the-one of the
surprises, as I mentioned,

43:08.206 --> 43:09.846 align:middle
was that banks that had told us

43:09.896 --> 43:13.526 align:middle
that their lowest comfortable
level of reserves was here.

43:13.526 --> 43:16.216 align:middle
They were well above that,
and yet they didn't deploy

43:16.216 --> 43:17.566 align:middle
that liquidity when there seemed

43:17.566 --> 43:20.146 align:middle
to be great opportunities
to do that.

43:20.196 --> 43:20.976 align:middle
That didn't happen.

43:20.976 --> 43:21.916 align:middle
So why is that?

43:21.916 --> 43:26.636 align:middle
And so, we're doing
careful analysis of that.

43:26.636 --> 43:28.356 align:middle
You ask, would we lower capital

43:28.356 --> 43:30.016 align:middle
and liquidity requirements
because of this?

43:30.066 --> 43:31.406 align:middle
I don't think so.

43:31.406 --> 43:32.856 align:middle
I don't think that's
where this goes.

43:32.856 --> 43:36.846 align:middle
I think where it may go, though,
is to look at, for example,

43:36.846 --> 43:40.726 align:middle
intraday liquidity, which
used to be a common thing.

43:40.726 --> 43:43.846 align:middle
It used to be a common thing for
banks to have intraday liquidity

43:43.846 --> 43:45.836 align:middle
from the Fed, what are
called "daylight overdrafts."

43:45.836 --> 43:47.336 align:middle
That's something we can look at.

43:47.686 --> 43:49.486 align:middle
Also, there are just
a few technical things

43:49.486 --> 43:52.496 align:middle
that we can look at that would
perhaps make the liquidity

43:52.496 --> 43:54.466 align:middle
that we have-which
we think is ample

43:54.546 --> 43:57.476 align:middle
in the financial
system-move more freely

43:57.516 --> 43:59.226 align:middle
and be more liquid, if you will.

43:59.736 --> 44:02.356 align:middle
And those are things we would
do, but only if we can do it

44:02.356 --> 44:04.436 align:middle
without compromising
safety or soundness

44:04.436 --> 44:05.326 align:middle
or financial stability.

44:07.416 --> 44:09.256 align:middle
NAOATSU AOYAMA.

44:12.056 --> 44:17.926 align:middle
Thank you very much,
Chairman Powell.

44:18.046 --> 44:22.486 align:middle
Naoatsu Aoyama from the Asahi
Shimbun, a Japanese newspaper.

44:23.666 --> 44:27.776 align:middle
Given this sustained level of
low inflation, even in-even

44:27.776 --> 44:32.636 align:middle
in this country, do you
think-so there seems

44:32.636 --> 44:34.856 align:middle
to be a higher possibility

44:34.856 --> 44:40.816 align:middle
that lower inflation
expectations will remain

44:40.816 --> 44:41.746 align:middle
going forward.

44:41.746 --> 44:47.306 align:middle
So do you think a Japan-like
situation, or "Japanification"

44:47.606 --> 44:50.696 align:middle
of chronically low inflation

44:50.746 --> 44:55.476 align:middle
and interest rates-the
U.S. is getting trapped

44:55.536 --> 44:59.136 align:middle
by the Japanification
of this situation?

44:59.286 --> 44:59.936 align:middle
Do you think so?

45:00.066 --> 45:00.836 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

45:01.036 --> 45:06.066 align:middle
So we-we, of course, have
watched the situation in Japan

45:06.066 --> 45:11.296 align:middle
and now the situation in
Europe, and we-we note

45:11.296 --> 45:14.436 align:middle
that there are significant
disinflationary pressures

45:14.966 --> 45:15.666 align:middle
around the world.

45:15.736 --> 45:17.266 align:middle
And we don't think
we're exempt from those.

45:18.326 --> 45:19.276 align:middle
But, of course, if you look

45:19.846 --> 45:21.876 align:middle
at our current inflation
performance,

45:23.006 --> 45:26.396 align:middle
it hasn't been anything
like what we've seen

45:26.486 --> 45:28.036 align:middle
in those other places.

45:28.096 --> 45:30.226 align:middle
But we don't think-again,
we don't think we're exempt

45:30.226 --> 45:34.396 align:middle
from those pressures,
and we are, therefore,

45:34.526 --> 45:38.426 align:middle
strongly committed to having
inflation expectations anchored

45:38.426 --> 45:40.216 align:middle
at the level that is consistent

45:40.216 --> 45:42.306 align:middle
with the symmetric 2
percent inflation objective.

45:43.386 --> 45:44.586 align:middle
That's what we're committed to,

45:44.586 --> 45:46.136 align:middle
and we'll use our
tools to achieve.

45:46.416 --> 45:48.876 align:middle
So we take-we take the
risk very seriously.

45:49.456 --> 45:50.896 align:middle
It's not-the risk isn't

45:50.896 --> 45:53.666 align:middle
that inflation might run a
couple tenths below 2 percent.

45:53.966 --> 45:58.046 align:middle
The risk is that what we've
seen is other economies getting

45:58.046 --> 45:59.796 align:middle
on a disinflationary path,

45:59.836 --> 46:01.366 align:middle
that it's been very hard
for them to get off.

46:01.426 --> 46:04.196 align:middle
Once inflation expectations
start sliding down,

46:04.426 --> 46:05.656 align:middle
inflation moves down.

46:05.986 --> 46:08.456 align:middle
You have less-interest
rates move down as well,

46:08.806 --> 46:11.086 align:middle
because there's an inflation
component in interest rates.

46:11.686 --> 46:15.266 align:middle
And, you know, we think that
the right thing to do is

46:15.266 --> 46:17.286 align:middle
to do what we can now to hold

46:17.626 --> 46:20.046 align:middle
and really move inflation
expectations up so

46:20.046 --> 46:22.346 align:middle
that they're squarely
and firmly anchored

46:22.626 --> 46:24.716 align:middle
at a level that's consistent
with 2 percent inflation.

46:25.216 --> 46:27.366 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

46:27.366 --> 46:32.106 align:middle
Hi. Brian Cheung
with Yahoo Finance.

46:32.106 --> 46:34.566 align:middle
Another type of balance
sheet question,

46:34.726 --> 46:37.816 align:middle
but not necessarily focused

46:37.816 --> 46:40.626 align:middle
on the repo crunch-there was
a discussion before that,

46:40.746 --> 46:43.736 align:middle
that maybe we should move-or,
rather, the Fed should move

46:43.736 --> 46:46.136 align:middle
to a more neutral balance
sheet, trying to remove some

46:46.136 --> 46:48.116 align:middle
of the agency mortgage-backed
securities that are

46:48.116 --> 46:49.876 align:middle
on the balance sheet
and maybe replace them

46:49.946 --> 46:51.556 align:middle
with shorter-term Treasuries.

46:51.556 --> 46:54.606 align:middle
Not any-I haven't really heard
an update on the composure

46:54.606 --> 46:56.966 align:middle
or the composition of the
balance sheet for right now.

46:57.266 --> 46:59.126 align:middle
I'm just wondering if the
Fed is actively thinking

46:59.126 --> 47:02.516 align:middle
about that right now, and,
if so, when we might expect

47:02.516 --> 47:03.306 align:middle
to hear something on that.

47:03.676 --> 47:03.976 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

47:03.976 --> 47:05.896 align:middle
You know, it's a-it's an
issue, but it's not an issue

47:05.896 --> 47:08.376 align:middle
that we're currently working on
and-or reaching a decision on

47:08.376 --> 47:09.116 align:middle
or anything like that.

47:09.116 --> 47:11.246 align:middle
The question of the
longer-run maturity composition

47:11.246 --> 47:13.076 align:middle
of the balance sheet
is a-is a big one,

47:13.076 --> 47:14.766 align:middle
and it's one we'll
return to over time,

47:14.766 --> 47:16.016 align:middle
but not-not imminently.

47:17.046 --> 47:17.796 align:middle
VIVIENNE NUNIS.

47:18.356 --> 47:22.806 align:middle
Chairman Powell, this
morning, the President tweeted

47:22.806 --> 47:25.486 align:middle
that we're seeing the greatest
economy in American history.

47:25.486 --> 47:27.986 align:middle
I just wondered, as the Chair
of America's central bank,

47:27.986 --> 47:29.576 align:middle
what you made of that
characterization.

47:30.036 --> 47:30.596 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

47:30.596 --> 47:32.786 align:middle
You know, I'm going to
maintain my longtime practice

47:32.786 --> 47:34.076 align:middle
of not-not commenting

47:34.076 --> 47:37.246 align:middle
on anything any elected official
would say, but thank you.

47:37.846 --> 47:38.696 align:middle
VIVIENNE NUNIS.

47:38.696 --> 47:40.906 align:middle
Okay, thank you.

47:41.906 --> 47:42.586 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

47:42.886 --> 47:46.796 align:middle
Thanks very much.

